WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.003 --> 00:00:07.170
GARRETT GROLEMUND: Thank
you everyone for attending.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.840
This will be the first in a
series of three webinars that

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.290
focuses on how to use
reproducibility in a business

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:14.240
setting.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:15.990
And the real gist of
what we're doing here

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:18.150
is over the past few
years, as you may know,

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:20.400
academics have
been very concerned

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.110
with the problem
of reproducibility

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:24.210
in data science research.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:25.830
Here's just one
of many headlines

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:28.620
that the public has read
about reproducibility.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:29.970
This one comes from Forbes.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:33.240
But there's been a, quote
unquote, reproducibly crisis

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:34.740
in data science research.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.180
And there's been
a lot of progress

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.190
made to address that crisis.

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.530
And from a technological
standpoint,

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:42.762
the problem's been
solved in my opinion.

00:00:42.762 --> 00:00:44.220
But there are many
cultural changes

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:46.185
that still need to be made.

00:00:46.185 --> 00:00:48.060
The cultural changes
don't concern us so much

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.130
as the technological solution.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:52.080
The new technology
that data scientists

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.120
are using to make
their work reproducible

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.150
has created
unintentional benefits

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:00.370
for people who use data
science in a business setting.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:02.312
And that's what these
webinars will look at.

00:01:02.312 --> 00:01:04.020
The first webinar,
which you're at today,

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:06.000
is Reproducibility
in Production.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.970
And I'm going to talk about
the technological solutions

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.410
to the reproducibly
crisis, specifically,

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:12.952
one type of technology
that I'm going

00:01:12.952 --> 00:01:15.180
to call Computational
Documents which

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:17.840
are just documents with
executable code inside of them.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.298
I'll show you how you can use
those to create opportunities

00:01:20.298 --> 00:01:23.100
both for yourself and the people
who consume your data science

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.250
intelligence, whether that's
customers, clients, bosses,

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:28.320
colleagues, so on.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.390
In the second webinar
on September 18,

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:33.450
Thomas Mock will talk about
RStudio Connect in Production.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:35.250
RStudio Connect is a
production platform

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.208
that allows you to share
the types of documents

00:01:37.208 --> 00:01:38.640
that we'll be
talking about today.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.320
And it really
completes the circle

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:44.190
in terms of providing
opportunities and advantages

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:46.620
in an everyday
business situation.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.270
In the final webinar,
Kelly O'Briant

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:51.240
will talk about Interactivity
which is probably the largest

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.790
opportunity created by the
technology of Computational

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:54.960
Documents.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.200
To go over the best practices
for making interactive material

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.450
and tell you the things that
you should be considering

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.660
as you develop interactive
material for others to consume,

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:07.850
that webinar will take
place on October 2.

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:10.759
So for today, let's
start by looking at what

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:12.650
Computational Documents are.

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:15.180
This is what I mean by
Computational Document.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:16.760
Let me exit the
slides for a moment.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:18.500
Go to RStudio.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:21.080
This is a
Computational Document.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:22.730
It's just a regular
document in a file.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:24.630
You can see text in here.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:29.870
But it has executable code
embedded throughout the file.

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:32.150
And a Computational
Document is just a document

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:34.710
that contains executable code.

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:38.190
And when you can put executable
code into a document,

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:41.390
then you can allow the reader
of the document to run the code.

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:43.790
And the code can
do things for them.

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:45.445
As someone who knows
how to write code,

00:02:45.445 --> 00:02:46.820
you could write
whatever code you

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:48.860
like to do whatever you
want the reader to be

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:50.855
able to do with your document.

00:02:56.630 --> 00:02:59.997
This document contains a data
analysis, a very simple one

00:02:59.997 --> 00:03:00.830
as a matter of fact.

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:02.828
And the code here just
recreates the analysis.

00:03:02.828 --> 00:03:05.120
And this is typical of what
Computational Documents do.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:07.340
They're a way to solve
reproducibility crisis

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:09.560
by putting the actual
reproducible data

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:11.095
analysis in your report.

00:03:11.095 --> 00:03:13.220
So whoever you pass the
report on to has everything

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.420
they need to recreate
the data analysis.

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:19.280
For example, I could run the
code that's in this document--

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:23.540
its buttons-- and reproduce
the document as I go.

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:26.540
So here, this analysis
is making some graphs.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:28.160
We'll look at them
in detail later.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:30.243
And then the other thing
that this particular type

00:03:30.243 --> 00:03:33.740
of document allows you to do
is you can Knit this report.

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:35.360
Both the text that
the author wrote

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:38.180
in the document and the
code that runs the document

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:39.007
is combined.

00:03:39.007 --> 00:03:41.090
And so we see the text
here with the code results.

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:42.980
And it creates a
finished presentation

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:45.690
to pass on to someone who
wants to know about our results

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:48.300
but maybe not about the code.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:51.080
So that's the gist of the
Computational Document.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:54.810
It is something that can
contain embedded code.

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.500
And because it can do
that, it can automatically

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:00.770
reproduce the data
analysis done with code.

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:03.380
And you can see here how
Computational Documents are

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:06.260
a definitive solution to the
reproducibility crisis in data

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:07.010
science.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:10.950
Everything we need to
reproduce our work is here.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:14.780
But because we're
reproducing it with code,

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:17.420
not only can we just simply
reproduce the analysis,

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:21.079
we can reproduce the
analysis automatically.

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:23.930
Now, automation is what
creates opportunities

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:27.320
for businesses who want to
use Computational Documents.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.660
And not only does it
create opportunities,

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:32.342
it's going to be a
disruptive technology that

00:04:32.342 --> 00:04:34.550
changes how you think about
delivering and performing

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:38.060
data science if you
deliver or perform

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:41.090
or conduct data science
as part of your job.

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:45.200
To put that in perspective, let
me draw a historical analogy.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:48.310
Go back to the slides.

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:50.340
It's the picture of
the printing press.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:52.140
The printing press,
famous example

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.780
of disruptive technology.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:57.120
When Johannes Gutenberg
introduce the printing press

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:00.180
into Europe in 1439,
no one can really

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:04.900
foresee the sort of changes that
it would catalyze in society.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:08.430
Now, the video here is stressing
the reproducible aspect

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:11.640
of the printing press, but
that's actually a red herring.

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:13.530
Monks can reproduce
books by hand

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:15.960
before anyone came along
with a printing press.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:18.390
What the printing press
did to change society

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:22.135
is it made the reproduction
of books automatable.

00:05:22.135 --> 00:05:23.760
It took a little bit
of initial set up.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:26.400
But after you did that,
you could automatically

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:28.170
reproduce books.

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:30.630
Now compare that to a
Computational Document.

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:32.430
It takes a little
bit of initial set up

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:34.680
to write the document,
to put the code you

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:36.420
want inside the document.

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:40.050
But after you do that set
up, reproducing the analysis

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:44.155
or reproducing the task that
the code does is automatable.

00:05:44.155 --> 00:05:45.780
You could pass a
document on to someone

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:47.410
who doesn't know
how to use code,

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:52.140
make it automatically do what
you design the document to do.

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:54.240
What sort of changes
should you expect

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:55.710
this to trigger downstream?

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:58.710
Well again, consider
the printing press.

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:02.250
When it became automatable
to reproduce books,

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:03.450
many things changed.

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:05.010
For the first time
in history, it

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:07.350
was now profitable to
create a book to sell.

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:10.050
And that gave rise to a huge
new industry, the publishing

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:13.470
industry, which is
still with us today.

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:15.720
For the first time
in history, authors

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:18.540
had considerable economic
and political power.

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:20.040
And that triggered new laws.

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:22.170
Copyright laws which
protected authors

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:25.050
from publishers and people
trying to steal their work,

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:27.780
and also censorship laws
which protected the public,

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:30.390
supposedly, from the authors.

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:34.110
Another thing that's very
prominent when we talk printing

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:36.180
press is for the
first time in history,

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:38.520
the Bible was no
longer a black box.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:41.490
People could look at it without
an intermediary in the form

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:43.050
of a priest or the church.

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:45.090
And they could develop
new interpretations

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:46.860
on what they were working with.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:48.810
And that led to new conflicts.

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:52.680
And then just more generally,
after it became automatable

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:57.450
to reproduce the sort of
work, it became beneficial--

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:58.800
perhaps for the first time--

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:02.160
for the common person
to learn how to read.

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:04.705
I think some of
these changes are

00:07:04.705 --> 00:07:06.580
things that we'll see
on a much smaller scale

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:09.270
as Computational Documents
become more and more used

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:10.050
in our fields.

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:15.300
There's two main
benefits Computational

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:17.340
documents that we're
going to focus on today.

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:19.350
And the first benefit
is for authors.

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:21.210
When you use
Computational Documents

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:23.970
to report your results and
to deliver the insights you

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:26.070
develop with your
data science, you

00:07:26.070 --> 00:07:29.670
can start reporting those
results much more efficiently.

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:32.550
You could reuse the same
initial work, the same setup

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:34.740
that you put into making
one document to create

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:36.910
many, many documents.

00:07:36.910 --> 00:07:39.060
The second change that
Computational Documents

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:41.620
facilitate is for users.

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:44.130
The people who
consume your reports

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:46.920
that you generate with
Computational Documents

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:49.770
can interact with the
content in new ways.

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:52.530
Ways that might be
very valuable to them.

00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:54.690
We thought they might
be very excited to have

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:56.795
and even willing to pay for.

00:07:56.795 --> 00:07:58.170
So we're going to
look at how you

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:00.300
can create Computational
Documents that you

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:05.480
can use in either of these
ways to make your life better.

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:07.580
For the remainder
of the webinar,

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:09.052
we'll look at R
Markdown which is

00:08:09.052 --> 00:08:10.760
a technology that
makes it easy to create

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:12.610
Computational Documents.

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:14.510
I'll give you some
tips about the features

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:16.640
of R Markdown that, I
think, have the largest

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:19.220
payoff for using
Computational Documents

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:21.810
in this sort of business
or production setting.

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:23.840
And then finally,
we'll think about how

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:26.510
to include interactivity or
not include interactivity

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:28.500
in the documents that you make.

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:30.840
Now, throughout this
webinar and the two webinars

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:32.429
that follow in the
series, we're going

00:08:32.429 --> 00:08:34.710
to talk about the
word "production."

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:37.020
And I want to be clear
up front that production

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:38.850
means different things
to different people,

00:08:38.850 --> 00:08:40.733
and we're aware of that.

00:08:40.733 --> 00:08:42.900
So please don't be distracted
by the word production

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:45.060
if it shows up in
these webinars.

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:48.270
But that we just mean that
these materials that we're

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:51.480
showing you are ready to use
however you want to use them

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:53.430
and with whoever you
want to use them with.

00:08:57.120 --> 00:08:57.970
All right.

00:08:57.970 --> 00:09:01.390
So we've looked at the promise
of Computational Documents.

00:09:01.390 --> 00:09:04.930
Now, let's look at a way for you
to make Computational Documents

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:07.210
with R. There are many
different ways to make

00:09:07.210 --> 00:09:08.470
Computational Documents.

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:13.090
We're going to focus on just
one known as R Markdown.

00:09:13.090 --> 00:09:16.600
And again I'll leave the slides
and get it to a demo here.

00:09:19.150 --> 00:09:21.310
The example document I
showed you earlier right

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:23.830
here is an R Markdown document.

00:09:23.830 --> 00:09:27.820
And R Markdown documents
are just plaintext files,

00:09:27.820 --> 00:09:30.665
but they contain three
different types of content.

00:09:30.665 --> 00:09:32.290
So if we look at this
file here, you'll

00:09:32.290 --> 00:09:35.020
see up here as if all
extension dot rmd.

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:36.250
Don't let that fool you.

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:39.310
Everything in this file
is just a character

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:41.450
and a plain character at that.

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:46.150
So this is a plaintext file that
makes it easy to edit, to save.

00:09:46.150 --> 00:09:51.100
If you wanted to use version
control on this file, say--

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:53.740
I have this file saved so if I
delete this and save it again.

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:56.940
I can come over here
to my RStudio tracker.

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:58.000
Go to webinar on the way.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:09:58.500
Follow this.

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:02.940
Oh, to rmd and I could diff it.

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:04.980
And since it's just
plaintext it's very easy

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:06.330
to read what's changed here.

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:08.340
This is a git diff.

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:11.730
And it's also easy to save on
GitHub and that sort of thing.

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:14.460
I'm not going to save this
change because it's not

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:15.540
a productive one.

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:17.790
But this file and the other
ones I'm showing you today

00:10:17.790 --> 00:10:19.410
will be available
at the shortly.

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:21.327
I'll share it again at
the end of the webinar.

00:10:21.327 --> 00:10:23.950
That contains the
materials for today.

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:25.890
So we're looking
at R Markdown file,

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:30.360
and we see that it
does have text here.

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:33.100
It also has code
as we saw before.

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:34.930
And it as a header
here which is just

00:10:34.930 --> 00:10:36.900
some metadata about this file.

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:39.870
The three types of
content are demarcated

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:41.580
by combinations of characters.

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:44.150
That's what allows us to
remain a plaintext file.

00:10:44.150 --> 00:10:45.457
So if you have--

00:10:45.457 --> 00:10:47.040
if you want to have
code in your file,

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:49.140
you can surround it
by three backticks.

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:51.637
And any program that's
aware of this file format

00:10:51.637 --> 00:10:53.220
will be able to
recognize that there's

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:55.355
executable code between
those backticks.

00:10:55.355 --> 00:10:56.730
If you want to
make a header, you

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:59.100
could surround it
by three dashes.

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:00.810
And if you use one
right plaintext well

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:02.310
you could just write plaintext.

00:11:02.310 --> 00:11:04.202
Although I have added
some things here

00:11:04.202 --> 00:11:05.160
we'll talk about a bit.

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:13.380
Since this is a
plaintext file, you

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:15.300
can open it in any text editor.

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:17.760
But I would recommend
opening it in RStudio editor

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:20.040
because RStudio is
designed in some ways

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:23.310
to be a text editor
for R Markdown files.

00:11:23.310 --> 00:11:25.200
The RStudio ID
will automatically

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:28.500
locate your code chunks in here
surrounded by the backticks.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:32.520
That allow you to play them in
that fashion I showed earlier.

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:35.520
You can use this as
you write your document

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:37.780
to make the document
effectively a notebook,

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:38.820
a notebook interface.

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:41.370
You can write your
code, test it out,

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:43.740
see if it does what you want
to make the results go away

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:44.890
feel like looking at them.

00:11:44.890 --> 00:11:46.348
But if you do like
looking at them,

00:11:46.348 --> 00:11:48.600
they appear right here
next to your code.

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:52.170
And then finally, the RStudio
ID provides a Knit button

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:53.640
at the top of your
document which

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:56.820
allows you to use this
plaintext document to generate

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:01.580
a new document that
contains the same content.

00:12:01.580 --> 00:12:06.042
At the moment, this document
set up to generate an html file.

00:12:06.042 --> 00:12:07.500
The html file has
everything that's

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:10.530
in the original document
including the code results.

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:13.453
In this case, I
asked for RStudio

00:12:13.453 --> 00:12:15.120
not to include the
code itself because I

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:16.443
thought I'd be distracting.

00:12:16.443 --> 00:12:18.360
And you can see this is
an html file like it's

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:19.890
shared on a website or not.

00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:22.860
RStudio opens up a preview
so I could see the results

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:23.790
and read them.

00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:25.800
And then if I look
down here, I will

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:27.690
see that the actual
file being previewed

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:29.340
exists in my directory.

00:12:29.340 --> 00:12:31.770
That's how I could take it
and share it and do something

00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:33.120
with it.

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:36.270
Like I said, this document is
set up to return html output.

00:12:36.270 --> 00:12:38.140
But I could use the
dropdown menu here,

00:12:38.140 --> 00:12:41.490
and I could create the
same content as a PDF file.

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:44.610
None of this really
affects the plaintext file.

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:46.570
It creates a new separate file.

00:12:46.570 --> 00:12:48.190
This file is a PDF file.

00:12:48.190 --> 00:12:50.370
So if I submitting to a
journal perhaps or going

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:53.010
to print this out, maybe I
would use the PDF versions

00:12:53.010 --> 00:12:54.420
than the html version.

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:57.720
And then also I can make
a Microsoft Word version.

00:12:57.720 --> 00:13:00.390
These are just some defaults
that come in RStudio

00:13:00.390 --> 00:13:01.620
for making these files.

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:03.300
But the idea behind
R Markdown is

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:05.790
you can use your plaintext
file to generate report

00:13:05.790 --> 00:13:08.480
in a limitless
number of formats.

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:10.230
Each report will contain
the same content,

00:13:10.230 --> 00:13:12.450
but it will be adapted
to a new format.

00:13:12.450 --> 00:13:16.200
In this website,
rmarkdown.rstudio.com/formats,

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:19.380
is an enumerated list of the
options you have available

00:13:19.380 --> 00:13:22.500
for changing your plaintext R
Markdown files into new types

00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:23.500
of files.

00:13:23.500 --> 00:13:26.430
Each of these are a
function or an R package

00:13:26.430 --> 00:13:28.350
that can handle an R
Markdown file and turn

00:13:28.350 --> 00:13:29.560
into something new.

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:33.030
You see you can make books,
websites, blogs, packages,

00:13:33.030 --> 00:13:35.760
no shortage of things
with R Markdown files.

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:37.610
I click through the
flexdashboard one.

00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:39.690
This is a way to make
interactive book, to make

00:13:39.690 --> 00:13:42.370
DI dashboards R Markdown files.

00:13:42.370 --> 00:13:45.720
And if we look at
some of the examples,

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:47.800
maybe click through
on one of them.

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:48.750
Here's the dashboard.

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:49.970
It's made with R Markdown.

00:13:49.970 --> 00:13:52.830
If we look at the source
code here, you can see--

00:13:52.830 --> 00:13:55.840
here's the familiar R Markdown
header separated by dashes.

00:13:55.840 --> 00:13:58.080
Here's some code chunks
separated by backticks.

00:13:58.080 --> 00:13:59.963
And there's some
formatting here that

00:13:59.963 --> 00:14:01.130
tells what you've been come.

00:14:06.220 --> 00:14:09.700
So to recap the
strength of R Markdown--

00:14:09.700 --> 00:14:11.200
they're just
plaintext files which

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:13.540
makes them very
portable, very usable.

00:14:13.540 --> 00:14:15.580
But you can embed code
in them which makes them

00:14:15.580 --> 00:14:18.870
a true Computational Document.

00:14:18.870 --> 00:14:21.345
When you author an R Markdown
file, you choose RStudio ID

00:14:21.345 --> 00:14:22.720
and you have a
notebook interface

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:26.190
if you like that experience
for testing out your code.

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:28.950
And then finally, the whole
idea behind R Markdown

00:14:28.950 --> 00:14:31.890
is that even though you're
working in a plaintext file,

00:14:31.890 --> 00:14:34.140
you can generate a report
that is not playing at all.

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:36.015
And in fact, the report
could be in a variety

00:14:36.015 --> 00:14:37.990
of different formats.

00:14:37.990 --> 00:14:41.880
So far in my example, I just use
the Knit button in R Markdown,

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:45.700
but it is worth noting
that you can also join

00:14:45.700 --> 00:14:46.950
these things programmatically.

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:50.640
There is a function in R
Markdown package called Render.

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:52.830
And if you send it the--

00:14:52.830 --> 00:14:54.490
fall path your file.

00:14:54.490 --> 00:14:57.328
So please help me here.

00:14:57.328 --> 00:14:59.370
It will render the file
for you programmatically.

00:14:59.370 --> 00:15:01.050
So you don't even
need RStudio ID.

00:15:01.050 --> 00:15:03.400
You just need an R terminal
to render these files.

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:05.130
If you do it
programmatically, RStudio ID

00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:06.570
isn't going to open a
preview or anything for you.

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:08.220
But the file will
be regenerated,

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:11.430
and it will be saved next
alongside your other file.

00:15:18.790 --> 00:15:22.050
So now you know the value
proposition of R Markdown.

00:15:22.050 --> 00:15:24.420
Let's look at the features
of R Markdown that

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:27.060
will be the most successful
for making Computational

00:15:27.060 --> 00:15:31.560
Documents to share with
consumers of a business nature.

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:34.740
I'm not going to show you every
single thing you need to learn

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:37.260
to create R Markdown files
because I already document all

00:15:37.260 --> 00:15:39.660
of this in self-paced
tutorial at this website,

00:15:39.660 --> 00:15:42.060
rmarkdown.rstudio.com/lesson
1.html.

00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:48.143
This tutorial takes
about 45 minutes

00:15:48.143 --> 00:15:50.310
to maybe 90 minutes if you
take your time to finish.

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:53.520
And when you finish it, you
will be an expert in R Markdown.

00:15:53.520 --> 00:15:54.815
It's very simple technology.

00:15:54.815 --> 00:15:56.190
You just have to
learn the rules,

00:15:56.190 --> 00:15:57.840
and they're easy to reapply.

00:15:57.840 --> 00:16:00.857
It take you about the length
of this webinar to learn this,

00:16:00.857 --> 00:16:02.940
but I'm not going to use
the length of the webinar

00:16:02.940 --> 00:16:03.440
to teach it.

00:16:03.440 --> 00:16:06.390
Instead I want to focus on the
more high-value features here.

00:16:06.390 --> 00:16:09.300
I'm also not going to go very
down into the weeds with R

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:12.735
Markdown because all the details
are documented at this website,

00:16:12.735 --> 00:16:17.580
bookdown.org/yihui/rmarkdown
which is the bookdown version

00:16:17.580 --> 00:16:19.620
of an actual book
available for purchase.

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:22.943
But the book is available in
its entirety for free online

00:16:22.943 --> 00:16:23.610
at this website.

00:16:23.610 --> 00:16:25.770
And it documents any
questions that you

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:28.170
might have about R Markdown.

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:29.970
Instead, what I'm
going to focus on

00:16:29.970 --> 00:16:33.990
are the features that I think
will pay off the most for you

00:16:33.990 --> 00:16:36.040
watching today.

00:16:36.040 --> 00:16:37.890
So first of all,
first feature, how

00:16:37.890 --> 00:16:39.420
do you create R Markdown file?

00:16:39.420 --> 00:16:41.128
If you want to create
an R Markdown file,

00:16:41.128 --> 00:16:45.210
go to the RStudio ID, click
File, New File, R Markdown.

00:16:45.210 --> 00:16:47.770
And when the wizard
pops up, just click OK.

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:50.767
This will open a file that
has a template inside it.

00:16:50.767 --> 00:16:52.350
You can read the
template if you want.

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:54.267
It tells you a little
about R Markdown format,

00:16:54.267 --> 00:16:56.370
but typically, you would
just delete everything.

00:16:56.370 --> 00:16:58.530
And start writing your
file, or you can just

00:16:58.530 --> 00:17:02.080
open a plaintext file and start
writing a file, same thing.

00:17:02.080 --> 00:17:05.484
So let's start writing a file.

00:17:05.484 --> 00:17:07.859
I'm going to try to recreate
the report that I showed you

00:17:07.859 --> 00:17:08.670
earlier.

00:17:08.670 --> 00:17:11.350
And to make it easier, I'm
just going to copy and paste

00:17:11.350 --> 00:17:12.475
some of the text over here.

00:17:15.750 --> 00:17:17.660
So I'm start my report.

00:17:17.660 --> 00:17:19.880
The graph below displays
the adverse events

00:17:19.880 --> 00:17:22.579
reported for a drug.

00:17:22.579 --> 00:17:26.790
This report, it's going
to look at prednisone.

00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:29.490
So I'm pretty sure I did
not spill right there.

00:17:29.490 --> 00:17:30.753
Well, maybe I did.

00:17:30.753 --> 00:17:32.670
And the most common side
effect of prednisone,

00:17:32.670 --> 00:17:35.460
I happen to know, is that
the drug is not always

00:17:35.460 --> 00:17:43.370
effective which, as side effects
go, is not the worst by far.

00:17:43.370 --> 00:17:44.232
So this is a report.

00:17:44.232 --> 00:17:45.440
This is a very simple report.

00:17:45.440 --> 00:17:47.240
I can write report
that goes on for pages.

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:50.420
But right now, this
will be enough for us.

00:17:50.420 --> 00:17:51.860
When you write
text in R Markdown,

00:17:51.860 --> 00:17:53.080
you can mark up the text.

00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:55.490
For example, if I put
asterisks around it,

00:17:55.490 --> 00:17:58.113
then RStudio will know that
I don't want those asterisks

00:17:58.113 --> 00:17:59.030
being on final report.

00:17:59.030 --> 00:18:01.730
But I want the words
between those asterisks--

00:18:01.730 --> 00:18:03.272
save this somewhere--

00:18:07.210 --> 00:18:10.010
I want those words to be
italicized in final report.

00:18:10.010 --> 00:18:14.560
And so here I have "adverse
events" are in italics.

00:18:14.560 --> 00:18:18.940
There's about two dozen signals
you could use to mark up

00:18:18.940 --> 00:18:20.363
the text in your final report.

00:18:20.363 --> 00:18:22.030
I'm not going to cover
it today, but you

00:18:22.030 --> 00:18:23.350
can learn it in the tutorial.

00:18:23.350 --> 00:18:27.250
That's why "adverse events" and
the asterisks for what it does.

00:18:27.250 --> 00:18:31.150
Instead, I want to focus you
on a more interesting way

00:18:31.150 --> 00:18:36.160
to change your text in reports.

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:38.500
You could type out your text
by hand which I've done,

00:18:38.500 --> 00:18:42.590
or you could use R code
to generate text for you.

00:18:42.590 --> 00:18:44.950
So first let's put
some R code in here.

00:18:44.950 --> 00:18:47.825
I'm going to just copy over the
set up chunk from the report

00:18:47.825 --> 00:18:48.700
I'm trying to create.

00:18:51.230 --> 00:18:53.180
Put it at the top
of the document.

00:18:53.180 --> 00:18:56.430
What this chunk does is it loads
some packages in the helper

00:18:56.430 --> 00:18:58.250
script that it makes
some functions there.

00:18:58.250 --> 00:19:00.710
It takes a drug, it
takes an age range,

00:19:00.710 --> 00:19:04.310
it queries the FDA'S
open FDA database

00:19:04.310 --> 00:19:07.250
to find out what adverse events
or side effects are associated

00:19:07.250 --> 00:19:09.140
with that drug, in
this case, prednisone,

00:19:09.140 --> 00:19:11.030
for people of this age range.

00:19:11.030 --> 00:19:14.420
And then it makes the data
set out of those things.

00:19:14.420 --> 00:19:17.000
Right here, the data
says called "events."

00:19:17.000 --> 00:19:19.970
If I want to find out what
the most common side effect is

00:19:19.970 --> 00:19:22.370
in the events data set--
that's where "drug ineffective"

00:19:22.370 --> 00:19:23.600
comes from--

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:26.270
I could look it up
myself and type it out,

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:29.600
or I could ask R
to find it for me.

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:30.560
So I do--

00:19:30.560 --> 00:19:32.627
I have a script, 01 scripts.

00:19:32.627 --> 00:19:33.710
It's in the webinar files.

00:19:33.710 --> 00:19:35.840
Contains all the
code I'm using today.

00:19:35.840 --> 00:19:37.250
It says a script.

00:19:37.250 --> 00:19:39.290
This might be how you
typically write R code.

00:19:39.290 --> 00:19:41.630
There's no reason not to
write your R code inside an R

00:19:41.630 --> 00:19:42.553
Markdown document.

00:19:42.553 --> 00:19:44.720
I just have it sort of here,
so I can copy and paste

00:19:44.720 --> 00:19:45.678
throughout the webinar.

00:19:47.860 --> 00:19:50.260
I ran this code earlier,
events dollar sign

00:19:50.260 --> 00:19:53.290
terms, so it looks at the term
column of the events data set.

00:19:53.290 --> 00:19:57.430
And it finds out which type of
event has the maximum count.

00:19:57.430 --> 00:20:00.040
So this returns,
which side effect

00:20:00.040 --> 00:20:02.050
is the most common
for this drug?

00:20:02.050 --> 00:20:05.050
If I want the results of
this to finish my code,

00:20:05.050 --> 00:20:08.260
I could surround
it by backticks.

00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:11.440
And then after the first back
to put the letter R and a space.

00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:15.010
Now when I knit this report,
the reports go run that code

00:20:15.010 --> 00:20:17.660
and inserts the results right
into my line of code here.

00:20:17.660 --> 00:20:19.450
So it says "drug
ineffective" because that

00:20:19.450 --> 00:20:23.600
was what you get if you run
this code around this coding

00:20:23.600 --> 00:20:24.100
console.

00:20:24.100 --> 00:20:25.940
Perhaps, you'll see that.

00:20:25.940 --> 00:20:28.360
That adds it right
into my report.

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:31.690
As long as my code
returns something

00:20:31.690 --> 00:20:34.222
that's like a number or a
piece of a character string

00:20:34.222 --> 00:20:35.680
that could be
inserted into a text,

00:20:35.680 --> 00:20:38.805
I can use that code to
finish the report for me.

00:20:38.805 --> 00:20:40.930
And the reader won't know
which parts of the report

00:20:40.930 --> 00:20:43.270
are hard written
in with real text.

00:20:43.270 --> 00:20:44.770
In which parts are
moving parts that

00:20:44.770 --> 00:20:46.420
can change as the
results of the code

00:20:46.420 --> 00:20:48.660
that generates them change.

00:20:48.660 --> 00:20:50.560
So that's the first thing.

00:20:50.560 --> 00:20:54.940
It sends a Markdown to make
all events a header here.

00:20:54.940 --> 00:20:57.670
If I want to put a
code chunk in here,

00:20:57.670 --> 00:21:00.850
I could just type
out the backticks.

00:21:00.850 --> 00:21:03.730
It's all just character
conventions after all.

00:21:03.730 --> 00:21:06.910
But I prefer to use a keyboard
shortcut which in this case

00:21:06.910 --> 00:21:12.310
is option shift I. Option
control I inserts it for me.

00:21:12.310 --> 00:21:14.500
Or you can go to the
Insert button up here,

00:21:14.500 --> 00:21:16.420
and it will insert
a code chink for you

00:21:16.420 --> 00:21:18.160
wherever you want to run.

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:20.770
An interesting thing
revealed by the Insert button

00:21:20.770 --> 00:21:24.340
is you don't have to
insert only R code chunks.

00:21:24.340 --> 00:21:28.270
You can run any type of code you
like in an R Markdown document.

00:21:28.270 --> 00:21:30.160
For example, I could
run Python code

00:21:30.160 --> 00:21:32.380
in my document with
the Python code chunk.

00:21:32.380 --> 00:21:34.240
You do need to tell
R what type of code

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:36.040
appears in the
code chunk, so you

00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:39.280
type the name of the
language inside curly braces

00:21:39.280 --> 00:21:41.230
after the three backticks.

00:21:41.230 --> 00:21:43.750
No, I'm not a Python user, so
I'm going to stick to R code

00:21:43.750 --> 00:21:44.260
today.

00:21:44.260 --> 00:21:46.600
But if you're interested in
using other types of coding

00:21:46.600 --> 00:21:49.900
on R Markdown document,
specifically Python code,

00:21:49.900 --> 00:21:52.810
I recommend that you check out
Sean Lopp's recent webinar,

00:21:52.810 --> 00:21:55.210
RStudio in Python, a Love Story.

00:21:58.380 --> 00:21:59.070
All right.

00:21:59.070 --> 00:22:01.470
Let's use this code
to do something.

00:22:01.470 --> 00:22:02.940
You know about some
code over here,

00:22:02.940 --> 00:22:06.330
and I will use this code to
make a plot or some ggplot

00:22:06.330 --> 00:22:07.950
to code that makes a plot.

00:22:07.950 --> 00:22:09.580
I'll put it in this code chunk.

00:22:09.580 --> 00:22:13.920
And if I knit the document, now
I added a plot to my report.

00:22:13.920 --> 00:22:17.110
But the code that makes the
plot also appears in the report.

00:22:17.110 --> 00:22:19.480
So this is my second tip.

00:22:19.480 --> 00:22:22.120
If your consumer
is not an R user,

00:22:22.120 --> 00:22:24.340
I wouldn't show them
the code in your report.

00:22:24.340 --> 00:22:28.900
It makes an experience that they
will process psychologically

00:22:28.900 --> 00:22:31.670
very different than if you
just make a plaintext report

00:22:31.670 --> 00:22:33.007
that they're used to reading.

00:22:33.007 --> 00:22:34.840
If you want to hide the
code in your report,

00:22:34.840 --> 00:22:40.000
you could do that by adding a
knitr chunk argument up here

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:41.860
after the R in your code chunk.

00:22:41.860 --> 00:22:43.810
And the argument I
suggest you consider using

00:22:43.810 --> 00:22:45.743
is echo equals false.

00:22:45.743 --> 00:22:47.410
This, will cause the
results of the code

00:22:47.410 --> 00:22:50.260
to appear in your report, but
it will hide the code itself

00:22:50.260 --> 00:22:51.250
from appearing.

00:22:51.250 --> 00:22:56.570
So now when I knit this
document, I have my chart,

00:22:56.570 --> 00:22:58.633
but I don't have the code
that creates the chart.

00:22:58.633 --> 00:23:00.800
And to be honest, normally
if you're making a graph,

00:23:00.800 --> 00:23:02.900
people don't care what code
you're using to make the graph.

00:23:02.900 --> 00:23:04.490
They just want to
inspect the graph.

00:23:04.490 --> 00:23:07.470
And that's what echo
equals false does for you.

00:23:07.470 --> 00:23:07.970
All right.

00:23:07.970 --> 00:23:11.152
So we're slowly
building up a report.

00:23:11.152 --> 00:23:12.610
It's probably a
bit of a whirlwind,

00:23:12.610 --> 00:23:15.370
but hopefully you're going to
sense that it doesn't take too

00:23:15.370 --> 00:23:16.930
much to write these reports.

00:23:16.930 --> 00:23:19.300
They're actually fairly simple.

00:23:19.300 --> 00:23:20.260
All right.

00:23:20.260 --> 00:23:24.350
Echo equals false isn't the
only knitr option to consider.

00:23:24.350 --> 00:23:27.230
But if you want to
write more options,

00:23:27.230 --> 00:23:28.870
you can just start
typing in the new R.

00:23:28.870 --> 00:23:31.990
You use tab completion for
a list of possible options.

00:23:31.990 --> 00:23:34.610
But learning about these
options is a bit tricky.

00:23:34.610 --> 00:23:37.600
I confess because
echo and code chunks,

00:23:37.600 --> 00:23:38.730
they're not our functions.

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:42.710
So you can't open an R help
page to learn about them.

00:23:42.710 --> 00:23:45.790
So what I recommend is that if
you want to learn about chunk

00:23:45.790 --> 00:23:50.260
options in R Markdown, go to
this website, yihui.name/knitr.

00:23:50.260 --> 00:23:55.090
And it contains basically the
help pages for every chunk

00:23:55.090 --> 00:23:56.560
option in R Markdown.

00:23:56.560 --> 00:23:58.300
This would be the
place to go to.

00:23:58.300 --> 00:24:01.120
It's the first result when you
Google R Markdown code chunks

00:24:01.120 --> 00:24:02.110
or knitr code chunks.

00:24:09.230 --> 00:24:12.770
Now that we have some code, you
could consider this a complete

00:24:12.770 --> 00:24:13.380
document.

00:24:13.380 --> 00:24:18.580
If we look at what we have
over here, run again--

00:24:18.580 --> 00:24:23.040
document says, the graph
below displays adverse events

00:24:23.040 --> 00:24:24.040
reported for prednisone.

00:24:24.040 --> 00:24:25.430
The most common of which
is drug ineffective.

00:24:25.430 --> 00:24:27.055
And here's the other
ones on the graph.

00:24:27.055 --> 00:24:29.440
This might be all your
consumers are interested in.

00:24:29.440 --> 00:24:31.152
And you could pass
this off to them.

00:24:31.152 --> 00:24:32.860
But maybe the consumer
gets sick and they

00:24:32.860 --> 00:24:34.750
go to the doctor who
prescribes a second drug.

00:24:34.750 --> 00:24:36.792
And they want to know what
the adverse events are

00:24:36.792 --> 00:24:37.390
for that drug.

00:24:37.390 --> 00:24:39.250
You could recreate
this analysis,

00:24:39.250 --> 00:24:42.290
create a report for them,
and send it off to them.

00:24:42.290 --> 00:24:44.290
But when you're
working with code,

00:24:44.290 --> 00:24:46.060
usually you don't want
to repeat yourself.

00:24:46.060 --> 00:24:48.010
One, because it's more work.

00:24:48.010 --> 00:24:50.500
Two, it invites the chance
to make more mistakes.

00:24:50.500 --> 00:24:52.270
But three and most
importantly, you're

00:24:52.270 --> 00:24:53.800
working with code
is normally not

00:24:53.800 --> 00:24:55.258
necessary to repeat yourself.

00:24:55.258 --> 00:24:56.800
And now that we've
written this code,

00:24:56.800 --> 00:24:59.590
it's not necessary for
us to repeat ourselves.

00:24:59.590 --> 00:25:01.660
We can reuse this report
in multiple places

00:25:01.660 --> 00:25:04.120
by adding report parameters.

00:25:04.120 --> 00:25:06.550
And to do that, first
we'll have to add a header.

00:25:06.550 --> 00:25:08.677
A header is a section
of the report that

00:25:08.677 --> 00:25:10.510
doesn't appear in the
finished document that

00:25:10.510 --> 00:25:14.710
allows you to enter information
like metadata that customizes

00:25:14.710 --> 00:25:16.150
how the reports generate.

00:25:16.150 --> 00:25:18.865
To make a header, surround
on each side by three dashes.

00:25:18.865 --> 00:25:20.740
It should come at the
start of your document,

00:25:20.740 --> 00:25:22.060
and it is a header.

00:25:22.060 --> 00:25:24.070
Here, you can do things
like set the default

00:25:24.070 --> 00:25:26.170
output for the document.

00:25:26.170 --> 00:25:27.640
This would be the
output that gets

00:25:27.640 --> 00:25:29.770
used if you call the
render function instead

00:25:29.770 --> 00:25:32.790
of choosing one up here.

00:25:32.790 --> 00:25:38.460
One of the things you can cite
here is a parameters field.

00:25:38.460 --> 00:25:41.080
You could learn how to write
yaml and headers in the R

00:25:41.080 --> 00:25:41.830
Markdown tutorial.

00:25:41.830 --> 00:25:43.450
So I'm not going over it.

00:25:43.450 --> 00:25:46.680
But you could see it's key
value pair separated by colons.

00:25:46.680 --> 00:25:48.170
I'm going to create a parameter.

00:25:48.170 --> 00:25:49.590
Going in at drug.

00:25:49.590 --> 00:25:55.560
And I'm going to give it an
initial value of prednisone

00:25:55.560 --> 00:25:57.400
or whatever you want to be.

00:25:57.400 --> 00:26:01.020
And now that I've created a
parameter in my header here,

00:26:01.020 --> 00:26:03.990
the code in my
document can access

00:26:03.990 --> 00:26:06.750
the value of the parameters
as if they were a list.

00:26:06.750 --> 00:26:09.390
And actually, I
apologize for it.

00:26:09.390 --> 00:26:12.320
Params is the field.

00:26:12.320 --> 00:26:15.270
Now my code, they can
access the param's list.

00:26:15.270 --> 00:26:18.500
Instead of having a hard
code prednisone here,

00:26:18.500 --> 00:26:21.440
I could just access
params dollar sign drug.

00:26:21.440 --> 00:26:23.780
And this will return the
value, prednisone because I

00:26:23.780 --> 00:26:25.250
set that value to drug here.

00:26:25.250 --> 00:26:27.050
If I want more, I'll
must be on that list.

00:26:27.050 --> 00:26:30.920
I could have-- name
whatever parameters I want

00:26:30.920 --> 00:26:34.600
and set them to whatever
I want them to be.

00:26:34.600 --> 00:26:37.320
I don't even have
to use them all.

00:26:37.320 --> 00:26:41.280
I do have to spell correctly,
so let's fix this one.

00:26:41.280 --> 00:26:44.482
Now that I've created some
parameters, if I go to knit,

00:26:44.482 --> 00:26:45.690
it's going to knit my report.

00:26:45.690 --> 00:26:47.940
It's going to use the default
value of the parameters,

00:26:47.940 --> 00:26:50.580
and I won't see any changes
not time about prednisone.

00:26:50.580 --> 00:26:52.950
But if I come down the
knit with parameters,

00:26:52.950 --> 00:26:55.380
I will have the option
before I knit the report

00:26:55.380 --> 00:26:56.940
to change either of
these parameters.

00:26:56.940 --> 00:26:58.440
So we've been looking
at prednisone,

00:26:58.440 --> 00:27:01.320
but let's look at a
different drug like Tylenol.

00:27:01.320 --> 00:27:02.880
Now I knit through report.

00:27:02.880 --> 00:27:05.760
Whatever params dollar sign
drug appears in my code,

00:27:05.760 --> 00:27:07.470
it will return Tylenol.

00:27:07.470 --> 00:27:09.810
If I wrote a report
in the correct way,

00:27:09.810 --> 00:27:13.680
this will change the entire
report to speak about Tylenol

00:27:13.680 --> 00:27:15.030
instead of prednisone.

00:27:15.030 --> 00:27:18.220
And you see here,
I miss some places,

00:27:18.220 --> 00:27:20.790
but the graph is
talking about Tylenol.

00:27:20.790 --> 00:27:25.560
And the most common effect
of Tylenol is nausea.

00:27:25.560 --> 00:27:27.930
It doesn't take much to
parameterize the whole report

00:27:27.930 --> 00:27:30.270
to use this new params
dollar sign drug.

00:27:30.270 --> 00:27:32.730
For example, down here
where I said prednisone,

00:27:32.730 --> 00:27:36.510
well, I could insert
some inline R code.

00:27:36.510 --> 00:27:38.510
That inline R code could
just return the results

00:27:38.510 --> 00:27:40.810
of params dollar sign drug.

00:27:40.810 --> 00:27:43.220
This inline R code is
actually pretty useful

00:27:43.220 --> 00:27:44.690
since it's creating
the drug name.

00:27:44.690 --> 00:27:48.920
And maybe I would use it
up here to create a title.

00:27:48.920 --> 00:27:53.310
And now when I knit
this document--

00:27:53.310 --> 00:27:55.100
I have prednisone here.

00:27:55.100 --> 00:27:56.370
I knit it with parameters.

00:27:59.200 --> 00:28:00.070
Stay with Tylenol.

00:28:02.580 --> 00:28:04.920
I get my Tylenol document.

00:28:04.920 --> 00:28:07.680
I can now use this document
in the similar context

00:28:07.680 --> 00:28:09.750
to describe any drug
in the database.

00:28:09.750 --> 00:28:12.108
I've automated that work
by putting parameters

00:28:12.108 --> 00:28:13.275
into my R Markdown document.

00:28:18.490 --> 00:28:21.070
Next, let's look at another
option created by the fact

00:28:21.070 --> 00:28:25.060
that there's executable
code in this document.

00:28:25.060 --> 00:28:27.090
There are two types
of code in R that

00:28:27.090 --> 00:28:30.630
return interactive components.

00:28:30.630 --> 00:28:32.430
One type is called htmlwidgets.

00:28:32.430 --> 00:28:34.832
Htmlwidgets are a
collection of R packages

00:28:34.832 --> 00:28:35.790
that contain functions.

00:28:35.790 --> 00:28:37.620
And the functions
build a JavaScript

00:28:37.620 --> 00:28:41.430
powered self-contained widget
normally to make a graph that

00:28:41.430 --> 00:28:43.440
has interactive properties.

00:28:43.440 --> 00:28:45.720
And then there's
the Shiny package

00:28:45.720 --> 00:28:50.825
which provides ways to
toggle in a live R session

00:28:50.825 --> 00:28:52.680
and to interact with
the R session that

00:28:52.680 --> 00:28:54.550
runs in the background
of your document.

00:28:54.550 --> 00:28:56.133
Let's look at each
of these separately

00:28:56.133 --> 00:28:58.615
because when you combine them
with R Markdown documents,

00:28:58.615 --> 00:29:00.990
you very quickly have a way
to create interactive reports

00:29:00.990 --> 00:29:03.310
for your consumers.

00:29:03.310 --> 00:29:05.772
Start with htmlwidgets,
the best place

00:29:05.772 --> 00:29:07.230
to learn about them
is the website,

00:29:07.230 --> 00:29:11.750
htmlwidgets.org, so let's there.

00:29:11.750 --> 00:29:14.880
Let's jump right
over to the gallery.

00:29:14.880 --> 00:29:18.390
Each of these entries here is
a different type of htmlwidgets

00:29:18.390 --> 00:29:20.670
that does a different thing.

00:29:20.670 --> 00:29:23.250
These widgets are sort of
pre-programmed to use out

00:29:23.250 --> 00:29:24.180
of the box.

00:29:24.180 --> 00:29:25.770
They all do their thing.

00:29:25.770 --> 00:29:28.770
And if that thing is what you
want, then it's your lucky day.

00:29:28.770 --> 00:29:30.540
You could just start
using the widget.

00:29:30.540 --> 00:29:33.082
If you want to do something that
an html which it doesn't do,

00:29:33.082 --> 00:29:34.623
you won't be able
to use htmlwidgets,

00:29:34.623 --> 00:29:35.880
so you'll have to use Shiny.

00:29:35.880 --> 00:29:37.590
We'll look at that in a minute.

00:29:37.590 --> 00:29:39.990
You can see that most
of these htmlwidgets

00:29:39.990 --> 00:29:42.960
are designed to make a drafts.

00:29:42.960 --> 00:29:50.050
For example, we can make a map
that we could toggle here, make

00:29:50.050 --> 00:29:52.100
key maps and read off values.

00:29:52.100 --> 00:29:53.620
But for our reports
since our graph

00:29:53.620 --> 00:29:55.162
is just fine the
way it is, I'm going

00:29:55.162 --> 00:29:59.230
to use the underrated
DataTables widget.

00:29:59.230 --> 00:30:01.700
What this widget does is
allow you to actually explore

00:30:01.700 --> 00:30:03.280
the raw data in a table.

00:30:03.280 --> 00:30:06.640
And to add it to my
plot, all I have to do

00:30:06.640 --> 00:30:08.960
is maybe put some
texts this side up.

00:30:08.960 --> 00:30:12.100
So this will be our appendix.

00:30:12.100 --> 00:30:14.514
The raw data is available.

00:30:17.400 --> 00:30:21.350
And then I'll borrow
some code from over here.

00:30:21.350 --> 00:30:22.730
I'll put it in a code chunk.

00:30:30.986 --> 00:30:36.070
Again using echo's equals
false through backticks.

00:30:36.070 --> 00:30:42.680
That my document-- and now as
long as I pass this document

00:30:42.680 --> 00:30:44.630
on in an html-based format--

00:30:44.630 --> 00:30:46.520
because this is a
JavaScript table--

00:30:46.520 --> 00:30:48.290
users can explore
the actual data

00:30:48.290 --> 00:30:51.412
they can sort by the different
variables in the data set.

00:30:51.412 --> 00:30:53.870
They could decide how much the
one I look at it once and so

00:30:53.870 --> 00:30:54.380
on.

00:30:54.380 --> 00:30:56.360
So it adds real
value to this report.

00:30:56.360 --> 00:30:59.210
And all I did was call a
function that already exists

00:30:59.210 --> 00:31:03.725
in R to insert this interactive
htmlwidget into my document

00:31:03.725 --> 00:31:06.420
at a certain place.

00:31:06.420 --> 00:31:08.370
Now like I said,
htmlwidgets are really

00:31:08.370 --> 00:31:10.330
good for doing what
they're designed to do,

00:31:10.330 --> 00:31:13.830
but they're not very flexible
for creating new functionality.

00:31:13.830 --> 00:31:17.520
On the opposite end of that
spectrum is the Shiny package.

00:31:17.520 --> 00:31:21.030
Shiny is designed to let you
do whatever you want to do

00:31:21.030 --> 00:31:23.040
in an interactive fashion.

00:31:23.040 --> 00:31:24.060
Go to Shiny website.

00:31:24.060 --> 00:31:26.790
Here's a very simple example
of Shiny up down here.

00:31:26.790 --> 00:31:28.830
You see, I have a drop
down menu that I could

00:31:28.830 --> 00:31:30.930
use to select different things.

00:31:30.930 --> 00:31:32.700
The graph output
created with our changes

00:31:32.700 --> 00:31:34.200
when I make
different selections.

00:31:34.200 --> 00:31:39.910
I can select different
dates, overlay smooth line,

00:31:39.910 --> 00:31:41.440
and do this map, the graph.

00:31:41.440 --> 00:31:44.920
The idea is the user gets
to control some inputs.

00:31:44.920 --> 00:31:47.560
The inputs are used behind
the scenes in R analysis.

00:31:47.560 --> 00:31:50.590
And the output of that analysis,
be the graph, the table, text

00:31:50.590 --> 00:31:53.110
or what have you, is
displayed to the user.

00:31:53.110 --> 00:31:56.320
And that changes as the user
changes various components

00:31:56.320 --> 00:31:58.910
here.

00:31:58.910 --> 00:32:01.580
That's a very simple
Shiny widget or Shiny app,

00:32:01.580 --> 00:32:02.960
but you can see
that you can make

00:32:02.960 --> 00:32:06.050
some more interesting and
complicated Shiny apps here.

00:32:06.050 --> 00:32:10.190
You normally design an html
page that sort of serves as a UI

00:32:10.190 --> 00:32:11.000
to the app.

00:32:11.000 --> 00:32:12.440
And then you set
up the R session

00:32:12.440 --> 00:32:15.230
in the code that runs
in the background.

00:32:15.230 --> 00:32:16.990
Well, you do that with R code.

00:32:16.990 --> 00:32:19.490
And since you can insert R code
into an R Markdown document,

00:32:19.490 --> 00:32:21.643
you could do that in R
Markdown document as well.

00:32:21.643 --> 00:32:23.810
Now I have a document that
does the same thing we've

00:32:23.810 --> 00:32:26.330
been looking at, but
it uses Shiny functions

00:32:26.330 --> 00:32:29.450
like renderPlot and whatnot.

00:32:29.450 --> 00:32:33.380
To make the document we were
just looking at interactive,

00:32:33.380 --> 00:32:37.840
this document is
now a Shiny app.

00:32:37.840 --> 00:32:40.190
A little bit trouble here.

00:32:40.190 --> 00:32:41.640
There you go.

00:32:41.640 --> 00:32:42.890
So here's a Shiny app.

00:32:42.890 --> 00:32:44.858
It's showing us
Tylenol's results.

00:32:44.858 --> 00:32:46.400
We also bring it
down by gender here.

00:32:46.400 --> 00:32:49.650
Here's our table at the en.

00:32:49.650 --> 00:32:54.845
We could change the age ranges
for Tylenol based on like this.

00:32:54.845 --> 00:32:57.596
See that the biggest
side effect for Tylenol

00:32:57.596 --> 00:32:59.300
is complete suicide.

00:32:59.300 --> 00:33:00.700
Didn't expect that.

00:33:00.700 --> 00:33:02.450
For older people is fatigue.

00:33:02.450 --> 00:33:07.783
And if we want to look at
another drug, let's say, Advil,

00:33:07.783 --> 00:33:08.950
we could type in right here.

00:33:08.950 --> 00:33:10.870
And the app almost
immediately changes.

00:33:10.870 --> 00:33:13.900
This is actually and
truly a Shiny app

00:33:13.900 --> 00:33:15.520
made from an R
Markdown document.

00:33:15.520 --> 00:33:17.260
You can see the document here.

00:33:17.260 --> 00:33:19.840
If we were to add the
flexdashboard formats

00:33:19.840 --> 00:33:23.080
to this app, we'd have
an R Markdown document.

00:33:23.080 --> 00:33:26.710
But this really looks
like a Shiny app now.

00:33:26.710 --> 00:33:29.490
So this is the dashboard
version of that app

00:33:29.490 --> 00:33:32.530
I had made through R Markdown.

00:33:32.530 --> 00:33:33.190
All right.

00:33:33.190 --> 00:33:35.080
So let's pause.

00:33:35.080 --> 00:33:37.510
I mean, there's
quite a bit there.

00:33:37.510 --> 00:33:42.800
I'd like to recap
what we looked at.

00:33:42.800 --> 00:33:45.755
When you're making Computational
Documents with R Markdown,

00:33:45.755 --> 00:33:47.630
the opportunities you
should pay attention to

00:33:47.630 --> 00:33:50.330
are actually generating
the text of the document

00:33:50.330 --> 00:33:52.820
with code using inline code.

00:33:52.820 --> 00:33:56.440
Because if you do
that, it will pay off

00:33:56.440 --> 00:33:58.330
when you parameterize
a document.

00:33:58.330 --> 00:34:00.980
Next, don't think
of it in terms of R.

00:34:00.980 --> 00:34:04.210
Don't think of it as a Python,
SAS, JavaScript, whatever

00:34:04.210 --> 00:34:05.290
you like to use.

00:34:05.290 --> 00:34:08.257
You can put that into
an R Markdown document.

00:34:08.257 --> 00:34:10.090
So more exotic languages
will take some work

00:34:10.090 --> 00:34:11.860
on your part on the back end.

00:34:11.860 --> 00:34:16.306
The things used for data science
like Python are ready to go.

00:34:16.306 --> 00:34:18.139
Hide the code from your
users if they're not

00:34:18.139 --> 00:34:20.840
looking for a code experience.

00:34:20.840 --> 00:34:24.050
And then add parameters to
reports to make them flexible.

00:34:24.050 --> 00:34:26.449
Your entire report becomes
like a function in R

00:34:26.449 --> 00:34:28.639
that takes some input
and returns some output.

00:34:28.639 --> 00:34:31.699
And all you have to do is change
the input to change output.

00:34:31.699 --> 00:34:33.980
If you're generating the
text with inline code,

00:34:33.980 --> 00:34:36.679
you're actually able to
change the results that

00:34:36.679 --> 00:34:41.340
are reported to the user as the
parameters themselves change.

00:34:41.340 --> 00:34:44.070
Consider adding htmlwidgets
for self-contained interactive

00:34:44.070 --> 00:34:45.270
pieces.

00:34:45.270 --> 00:34:49.230
And then consider going to
adding Shiny components if you

00:34:49.230 --> 00:34:51.239
need to do some sort
of custom interactions

00:34:51.239 --> 00:34:54.719
based on your
unique R algorithms.

00:34:54.719 --> 00:34:56.935
If you look at these
things that we went over,

00:34:56.935 --> 00:34:59.310
you'll notice here at the end
of these last three options

00:34:59.310 --> 00:35:01.830
are sort of like a
hierarchy of ways

00:35:01.830 --> 00:35:04.850
to make your report more
and more interactive.

00:35:04.850 --> 00:35:07.640
And you could think of this
hierarchy of interactivity

00:35:07.640 --> 00:35:10.790
from static R Markdown
documents I wanted to Shiny apps

00:35:10.790 --> 00:35:16.070
on the other end which are
completely interactive tools.

00:35:16.070 --> 00:35:18.390
All of these are
available for you to use.

00:35:18.390 --> 00:35:20.318
And so now, as
someone sophisticated

00:35:20.318 --> 00:35:21.860
who can make
Computational Documents,

00:35:21.860 --> 00:35:24.560
you need to start thinking
about your strategy

00:35:24.560 --> 00:35:27.140
for supplying needs using
these different levels

00:35:27.140 --> 00:35:28.130
of interactivity.

00:35:28.130 --> 00:35:32.880
So let's look at that,
thinking about interactivity.

00:35:32.880 --> 00:35:34.890
Not only does enter
wrong on this hierarchy

00:35:34.890 --> 00:35:37.790
create a more
interactive document,

00:35:37.790 --> 00:35:39.980
it also requires more
work on your part.

00:35:39.980 --> 00:35:41.630
Interactivity's
complicated to make.

00:35:41.630 --> 00:35:43.130
And when it come
to making something

00:35:43.130 --> 00:35:45.950
like a Shiny app, those of
you who have made Shiny apps,

00:35:45.950 --> 00:35:48.190
you know that there is
a lot going on there.

00:35:48.190 --> 00:35:49.940
And that is a very
specialized undertaking

00:35:49.940 --> 00:35:52.770
to make a Shiny app.

00:35:52.770 --> 00:35:55.200
But you don't have to make
a Shiny up every time you

00:35:55.200 --> 00:35:56.760
want interactivity.

00:35:56.760 --> 00:35:59.550
For example, if you just want
to generate the same report put

00:35:59.550 --> 00:36:01.770
on a new drug or on
a new market segment

00:36:01.770 --> 00:36:04.295
or on a new updated
database, you

00:36:04.295 --> 00:36:06.420
might be able to do that
with the simple R Markdown

00:36:06.420 --> 00:36:10.103
document that's been
parameterize with parameters.

00:36:10.103 --> 00:36:12.270
Alternatively, if you want
your user be able to poke

00:36:12.270 --> 00:36:15.042
and prod the document, you
might want to create a Shiny app

00:36:15.042 --> 00:36:17.250
or you might be fine with
an R Markdown document that

00:36:17.250 --> 00:36:19.860
uses htmlwidgets or
even an R Markdown

00:36:19.860 --> 00:36:21.390
document with Shiny components.

00:36:24.230 --> 00:36:26.990
What I recommend is that you aim
for the level of interactivity

00:36:26.990 --> 00:36:28.940
that's as simple as
possible to do the task

00:36:28.940 --> 00:36:31.670
that you want because that
leaves less room for things

00:36:31.670 --> 00:36:34.118
to go wrong and need to be
debugged on your part, less

00:36:34.118 --> 00:36:36.243
room for things that go
wrong and for the user app,

00:36:36.243 --> 00:36:38.420
of course, experience
and less time needed

00:36:38.420 --> 00:36:40.027
to create these documents.

00:36:40.027 --> 00:36:42.110
Here's some simple bright
lines that you could use

00:36:42.110 --> 00:36:44.570
to think about this hierarchy.

00:36:44.570 --> 00:36:50.560
First of all, if
you ask yourself,

00:36:50.560 --> 00:36:55.660
how many times do I need to
change the data in my report

00:36:55.660 --> 00:36:58.443
during my presentation or during
the viewer's presentation?

00:36:58.443 --> 00:36:59.860
How many times are
they going need

00:36:59.860 --> 00:37:01.990
to change the data
for themselves?

00:37:01.990 --> 00:37:04.570
If it's less than once,
then you don't need

00:37:04.570 --> 00:37:06.615
anything that involves Shiny.

00:37:06.615 --> 00:37:07.990
This is a very
important question

00:37:07.990 --> 00:37:10.540
to ask yourself
because we tend to see

00:37:10.540 --> 00:37:13.480
people learn Shiny and
then use it for everything.

00:37:13.480 --> 00:37:15.940
It's very hard to walk
back from Shiny once you've

00:37:15.940 --> 00:37:17.350
adopted a Shiny workflow.

00:37:17.350 --> 00:37:17.940
And that's OK.

00:37:17.940 --> 00:37:19.480
Shiny is excellent.

00:37:19.480 --> 00:37:21.640
But it's very easy
to overestimate

00:37:21.640 --> 00:37:24.160
what you need to accomplish
the results you want.

00:37:24.160 --> 00:37:26.950
And if you're not updating
your data more than once

00:37:26.950 --> 00:37:30.460
per session, then you can
just use a simple non-Shiny R

00:37:30.460 --> 00:37:32.890
Markdown document.

00:37:32.890 --> 00:37:35.590
And you can create a new
document before each session.

00:37:35.590 --> 00:37:38.680
And that document will
suffice for that session.

00:37:38.680 --> 00:37:42.090
The next thing to ask is, do
you need to create a custom

00:37:42.090 --> 00:37:44.970
UI for your app?

00:37:44.970 --> 00:37:47.190
If what you want to
report to your user

00:37:47.190 --> 00:37:50.715
would do fine in a document
format or a dashboard format,

00:37:50.715 --> 00:37:52.590
you could do that within
R Markdown document.

00:37:52.590 --> 00:37:56.130
And you don't need to invest in
creating a Shiny or an html UI

00:37:56.130 --> 00:37:58.080
to contain your
interactive components.

00:38:00.930 --> 00:38:03.090
All right.

00:38:03.090 --> 00:38:06.570
That is Computational
Documents in a nutshell.

00:38:06.570 --> 00:38:08.070
And hopefully from
there, you can

00:38:08.070 --> 00:38:11.190
see how you could use some
of the benefits I mentioned.

00:38:11.190 --> 00:38:13.050
I think the case for
generating reports

00:38:13.050 --> 00:38:15.480
efficiently with parameterized
R Markdown reports source

00:38:15.480 --> 00:38:16.710
speaks for itself.

00:38:16.710 --> 00:38:18.570
And then adding
interactive components,

00:38:18.570 --> 00:38:21.930
either through parameters
or htmlwidgets or Shiny

00:38:21.930 --> 00:38:25.860
components, will be the
way to empower your users

00:38:25.860 --> 00:38:27.640
to do different things.

00:38:27.640 --> 00:38:31.560
But we've really only covered
half of the user story.

00:38:31.560 --> 00:38:34.740
Computational Documents
create the opportunity

00:38:34.740 --> 00:38:36.480
to give your users
a document that can

00:38:36.480 --> 00:38:38.910
run code and interact for them.

00:38:38.910 --> 00:38:40.860
But whether or not
that opportunity

00:38:40.860 --> 00:38:43.110
will exist at the
user's fingertips

00:38:43.110 --> 00:38:47.200
will depend on how you share
that document with their user.

00:38:47.200 --> 00:38:50.320
The platform you use
to share the document

00:38:50.320 --> 00:38:52.300
will need to be able
to run and execute

00:38:52.300 --> 00:38:54.210
the code in your
Computational Document

00:38:54.210 --> 00:38:55.960
if you want the user
to be able to execute

00:38:55.960 --> 00:38:59.120
that code by interacting
with the document.

00:38:59.120 --> 00:39:01.120
And that's what the next
webinar will focus on.

00:39:01.120 --> 00:39:03.860
On September 18, we'll
look at RStudio Connect

00:39:03.860 --> 00:39:07.060
which is a production platform
for sharing data science

00:39:07.060 --> 00:39:07.825
products.

00:39:07.825 --> 00:39:10.450
Among the things that you could
share and RStudio Connect are R

00:39:10.450 --> 00:39:12.910
Markdown documents
and Shiny apps.

00:39:12.910 --> 00:39:17.650
In short, you can share your R--
your Computational Documents.

00:39:17.650 --> 00:39:20.380
Computational Documents create
a lot of new opportunities

00:39:20.380 --> 00:39:22.930
for letting people interact
with the things that you make.

00:39:22.930 --> 00:39:24.910
And RStudio Connect is
built to take advantage

00:39:24.910 --> 00:39:28.750
of each of those opportunities.

00:39:28.750 --> 00:39:33.100
To give you a taste of
what's happening, last--

