00:42:32.705 --> 00:42:34.248
It is one of the formats.

00:42:34.248 --> 00:42:36.540
How do we handle reproducibility
when packaged versions

00:42:36.540 --> 00:42:37.770
changes--

00:42:37.770 --> 00:42:40.920
package version changes may
cause results to change?

00:42:40.920 --> 00:42:45.440
Can we force a certain
version of each package?

00:42:45.440 --> 00:42:50.240
Yes, you can do that
depending on your use case.

00:42:50.240 --> 00:42:53.510
If you have a budget, if you
work for a company that's

00:42:53.510 --> 00:42:56.840
supporting your work
with R professionally,

00:42:56.840 --> 00:42:59.870
then you might look into the
RStudio Package Manager which

00:42:59.870 --> 00:43:02.390
is a tool designed
specifically to do this

00:43:02.390 --> 00:43:07.530
to help your group keep track
of which versions of packages

00:43:07.530 --> 00:43:08.840
are being used for what.

00:43:08.840 --> 00:43:11.280
It makes it very
simple to reproducibly

00:43:11.280 --> 00:43:13.110
track which packages
should be used when

00:43:13.110 --> 00:43:16.880
and then to create
libraries of those packages

00:43:16.880 --> 00:43:18.360
to use with certain projects.

00:43:18.360 --> 00:43:21.750
You can learn more about RStudio
Package Manager at RStudio.com.

00:43:21.750 --> 00:43:24.450
It's part of the new
RStudio team bundle.

00:43:24.450 --> 00:43:28.620
If you're working as an
individual, depending

00:43:28.620 --> 00:43:30.233
on your work and
how you use it, you

00:43:30.233 --> 00:43:31.650
might find RStudio
Package Manager

00:43:31.650 --> 00:43:33.690
useful to you as
well-- again, if you're

00:43:33.690 --> 00:43:35.430
an academic collab or whatnot.

00:43:35.430 --> 00:43:38.070
But if you don't want to
use RStudio Package Manager,

00:43:38.070 --> 00:43:41.130
you can check out some of the
open source solutions that sort

00:43:41.130 --> 00:43:45.540
of get at this problem like
the R EMV package R E-M-V

00:43:45.540 --> 00:43:54.020
and the pre-existing packrat
package, P-A-C-K-R-A-T.

00:43:54.020 --> 00:43:57.530
Handling changes in versions
and open source software

00:43:57.530 --> 00:43:59.570
is probably the
trickiest problem

00:43:59.570 --> 00:44:02.270
related to reproducibility when
you use open source software.

00:44:02.270 --> 00:44:04.952
And there are many people
working on this problem.

00:44:04.952 --> 00:44:06.410
I recommend RStudio
Package Manager

00:44:06.410 --> 00:44:09.290
because I know it's a very
professional solution that

00:44:09.290 --> 00:44:10.040
will work.

00:44:10.040 --> 00:44:12.373
But obviously, when you talk
about open source software,

00:44:12.373 --> 00:44:15.400
we're not always looking
for professional solutions.

00:44:15.400 --> 00:44:17.620
Can you please repeat the
difference between R setup

00:44:17.620 --> 00:44:20.230
versus R for the code chunks.

00:44:20.230 --> 00:44:21.890
Yes.

00:44:21.890 --> 00:44:24.200
And let's go to the code
and take a look at that.

00:44:27.380 --> 00:44:30.040
If I go back to the one
last developing here,

00:44:30.040 --> 00:44:33.030
the first chunk has
the word setup in it ,

00:44:33.030 --> 00:44:35.370
and the other chunks don't.

00:44:35.370 --> 00:44:37.690
You can give any chunk
you want a label.

00:44:37.690 --> 00:44:39.030
So I might call this one graph.

00:44:39.030 --> 00:44:41.460
They'll help me remember
that this is the chart that

00:44:41.460 --> 00:44:42.670
contains the graph.

00:44:42.670 --> 00:44:44.760
And if you're
inside RStudio, down

00:44:44.760 --> 00:44:47.688
share you can navigate the
chunks like chunk 1, 2, 3.

00:44:47.688 --> 00:44:49.230
If I use the labels,
it's much easier

00:44:49.230 --> 00:44:50.280
to figure out which
chunk I'm looking

00:44:50.280 --> 00:44:51.900
for because the
labels show up here.

00:44:51.900 --> 00:44:55.020
They're kind of used
in other places to.

00:44:55.020 --> 00:44:57.120
But R Markdown
itself will use one

00:44:57.120 --> 00:44:58.500
of the labels in a special way.

00:44:58.500 --> 00:45:02.100
If you name a chunk,
"setup," then whenever

00:45:02.100 --> 00:45:05.070
you try to run any code
in this document-- so this

00:45:05.070 --> 00:45:06.210
was a brand new document.

00:45:06.210 --> 00:45:07.500
I just opened it up.

00:45:07.500 --> 00:45:09.510
You can see here
in the setup chunk,

00:45:09.510 --> 00:45:11.550
I'm sourcing some
needed functions.

00:45:11.550 --> 00:45:15.920
I'm creating the data set that's
needed down here for events.

00:45:15.920 --> 00:45:17.490
Here's where events is created.

00:45:17.490 --> 00:45:19.860
But you can imagine--

00:45:19.860 --> 00:45:20.880
excuse me second.

00:45:24.490 --> 00:45:26.798
You could imagine sharing
this file with a colleague

00:45:26.798 --> 00:45:28.340
and they just want
to make the graph.

00:45:28.340 --> 00:45:29.450
They're excited
to make the graph.

00:45:29.450 --> 00:45:30.408
So they come down here.

00:45:30.408 --> 00:45:31.250
They open the file.

00:45:31.250 --> 00:45:32.410
They don't create the data.

00:45:32.410 --> 00:45:34.240
They don't run any of
that code up there.

00:45:34.240 --> 00:45:36.620
They just try to
create the graph.

00:45:36.620 --> 00:45:38.180
Well, if events
doesn't exist, they

00:45:38.180 --> 00:45:40.140
would get an error message.

00:45:40.140 --> 00:45:43.213
But if you put events
or anything else

00:45:43.213 --> 00:45:44.630
in the setup chunk,
then no matter

00:45:44.630 --> 00:45:46.850
what chunk gets run
first, R Markdown

00:45:46.850 --> 00:45:51.100
will ensure that the setup
chunk is run before that chunk.

00:45:51.100 --> 00:45:54.920
So the setup chunk is an ideal
place to load your packages,

00:45:54.920 --> 00:45:57.770
to download data if the
report relies on it,

00:45:57.770 --> 00:46:01.190
to set up intermedia objects
that the chunks flow rely on.

00:46:01.190 --> 00:46:05.090
The setup chunk will
always be run at least one

00:46:05.090 --> 00:46:07.440
time before anything else
in the document gets run.

00:46:07.440 --> 00:46:11.570
And that's why I use the
setup chunk label here.

00:46:11.570 --> 00:46:13.730
How does R Markdown
handle htmlwidgets

00:46:13.730 --> 00:46:16.970
when rendering into
a PDF and html?

00:46:16.970 --> 00:46:17.630
By Edgar.

00:46:17.630 --> 00:46:19.170
That's a great question, Edgar.

00:46:19.170 --> 00:46:21.770
Htmlwidgets actually
return JavaScript.

00:46:21.770 --> 00:46:23.450
They return a JavaScript widget.

00:46:23.450 --> 00:46:26.630
And if you put that into an
html document which is then

00:46:26.630 --> 00:46:28.910
presented by a web
browser, they'll

00:46:28.910 --> 00:46:31.275
know what to do with
that JavaScript widget.

00:46:31.275 --> 00:46:32.720
And it's going to work.

00:46:32.720 --> 00:46:34.970
And there are multiple
R Markdown formats

00:46:34.970 --> 00:46:36.230
that rely on html.

00:46:36.230 --> 00:46:39.290
There is ioslides presentations
or other types of slideshows,

00:46:39.290 --> 00:46:41.030
blogdown, bookdown.

00:46:41.030 --> 00:46:44.720
And anything that's html based,
htmlwidgets will work in.

00:46:44.720 --> 00:46:46.160
But htmlwidgets.

00:46:46.160 --> 00:46:49.080
Are not PDF widgets.

00:46:49.080 --> 00:46:52.590
PDF files are largely
static, and they

00:46:52.590 --> 00:46:53.790
don't recognize htmlwidgets.

00:46:53.790 --> 00:46:56.230
So if you were to output
that file to a PDF,

00:46:56.230 --> 00:46:57.870
the htmlwidget would not appear.

00:46:57.870 --> 00:47:00.190
You would not be able to
have an interactive PDF.

00:47:00.190 --> 00:47:02.790
You can think of PDFs
really for printing.

00:47:02.790 --> 00:47:05.400
And anything you
print out is not

00:47:05.400 --> 00:47:09.030
going to be very interactive
unless Origami's involved,

00:47:09.030 --> 00:47:11.720
I guess.

00:47:11.720 --> 00:47:14.780
How would you generate both
reports as separate documents

00:47:14.780 --> 00:47:16.400
but not edit/run each time.

00:47:20.470 --> 00:47:20.970
All right.

00:47:20.970 --> 00:47:24.162
So I guess, we have a
parameterized report.

00:47:24.162 --> 00:47:25.620
And we're talking
about prednisone,

00:47:25.620 --> 00:47:28.140
we're talking about Tylenol.

00:47:28.140 --> 00:47:31.486
If I go back to RStudio, this
is the parameterized version

00:47:31.486 --> 00:47:32.260
of the report.

00:47:32.260 --> 00:47:33.510
It's called 07 temp.

00:47:33.510 --> 00:47:35.890
I'm going to open
the console here.

00:47:35.890 --> 00:47:37.890
Because if I want to
generate both those reports

00:47:37.890 --> 00:47:41.340
and not to have to use the
Knit button or whatever,

00:47:41.340 --> 00:47:46.440
I would use the R
Markdown render function.

00:47:46.440 --> 00:47:54.547
I would pass it this
07-temp.rmd document.

00:47:54.547 --> 00:47:55.380
This will render it.

00:47:55.380 --> 00:47:57.020
If I just render
it like this, it's

00:47:57.020 --> 00:48:01.550
going to use the default values
for these params, parameters

00:48:01.550 --> 00:48:04.310
up here.

00:48:04.310 --> 00:48:05.180
Then let me--

00:48:11.240 --> 00:48:13.300
But let me consult
the help page,

00:48:13.300 --> 00:48:17.180
so I don't give out
any incorrect advice.

00:48:17.180 --> 00:48:19.010
I could set the
parameter values that I

00:48:19.010 --> 00:48:25.280
want to use in the R
Markdown render call.

00:48:34.840 --> 00:48:36.590
Right here with the
params argument.

00:48:36.590 --> 00:48:38.230
So this is params or so.

00:48:38.230 --> 00:48:42.040
If I go in here, might
double colon in there.

00:48:42.040 --> 00:48:43.570
Let me go to any other way.

00:48:43.570 --> 00:48:44.990
Set params.

00:48:44.990 --> 00:48:47.200
I goes to a list.

00:48:47.200 --> 00:48:53.470
And here, I could say, I
want a drug equal to Tylenol.

00:48:53.470 --> 00:48:56.560
And if I cared about doctor,
I don't actually use that.

00:48:56.560 --> 00:48:58.930
I would set that here, too.

00:48:58.930 --> 00:49:00.780
Close.

00:49:00.780 --> 00:49:02.830
Doctor.

00:49:02.830 --> 00:49:04.630
And then when I
run this document,

00:49:04.630 --> 00:49:07.060
it will use those
parameters there.

00:49:07.060 --> 00:49:11.610
I can also set out
the output file name.

00:49:11.610 --> 00:49:22.540
Set that to output file equals
07 Tylenol or something dot

00:49:22.540 --> 00:49:24.340
rmd.

00:49:24.340 --> 00:49:26.350
OK, so how that
answer your question?

00:49:26.350 --> 00:49:28.720
No, but it set up the solution.

00:49:28.720 --> 00:49:31.210
If you can generate this
document programmatically

00:49:31.210 --> 00:49:34.150
like this, you can
write a for loop that

00:49:34.150 --> 00:49:35.750
calls this a number of times.

00:49:35.750 --> 00:49:38.890
And each time it calls it,
it passes in a different set

00:49:38.890 --> 00:49:40.000
of parameters.

00:49:40.000 --> 00:49:43.510
And it passes a different
fall path to save results at.

00:49:43.510 --> 00:49:45.850
And by the time that for
loop finishes running,

00:49:45.850 --> 00:49:49.108
you'll have i
different documents.

00:49:49.108 --> 00:49:50.775
Each uses different
parameters, and each

00:49:50.775 --> 00:49:51.910
is saved in its own place.

00:49:51.910 --> 00:49:54.160
And that's how you can
generate 100 reports right away

00:49:54.160 --> 00:49:55.410
from a parameterized document.

