00:50:39.243 --> 00:50:40.910
What is the best way
to start learning R

00:50:40.910 --> 00:50:43.310
for experienced programmers?

00:50:43.310 --> 00:50:44.930
I think the book,
R for Data Science,

00:50:44.930 --> 00:50:46.347
would be the place
to start if you

00:50:46.347 --> 00:50:48.780
want to use R to analyze data.

00:50:48.780 --> 00:50:52.702
But if you want to understand
R as a language using what

00:50:52.702 --> 00:50:54.410
you already know about
software languages

00:50:54.410 --> 00:50:56.000
as an experienced
programmer, then I'd

00:50:56.000 --> 00:50:57.875
recommend starting with
the book, Advanced R,

00:50:57.875 --> 00:50:58.970
by Hadley Wickham.

00:50:58.970 --> 00:51:01.055
Both R for Data
Science and Advanced R

00:51:01.055 --> 00:51:03.230
are available for
free online, or you

00:51:03.230 --> 00:51:06.430
could purchase a print copy.

00:51:06.430 --> 00:51:08.180
When you have
interactivity, does the data

00:51:08.180 --> 00:51:10.880
have to be on the server or
available for general user

00:51:10.880 --> 00:51:12.530
to interact with?

00:51:12.530 --> 00:51:14.480
That's a good
question because it

00:51:14.480 --> 00:51:18.530
gets to an important
nuance between Shiny

00:51:18.530 --> 00:51:20.660
and non-Shiny things.

00:51:20.660 --> 00:51:21.730
I go back to the slides.

00:51:21.730 --> 00:51:24.590
Before we're looking
about the slide here.

00:51:24.590 --> 00:51:28.130
Shiny is a way to have
an R session running

00:51:28.130 --> 00:51:30.560
in the background that the
user can interact with.

00:51:30.560 --> 00:51:32.630
And that allows
all sorts of things

00:51:32.630 --> 00:51:35.455
because the user at any
moment can do anything

00:51:35.455 --> 00:51:37.580
that R can do as long as
you provide a way for them

00:51:37.580 --> 00:51:38.810
to do that.

00:51:38.810 --> 00:51:41.780
And Shiny is the only technology
that allows that to happen.

00:51:41.780 --> 00:51:44.810
Htmlwidgets do not.

00:51:44.810 --> 00:51:45.680
Parameters do not.

00:51:45.680 --> 00:51:48.080
Parameters will use R
to create the document,

00:51:48.080 --> 00:51:49.193
and then R goes away.

00:51:49.193 --> 00:51:51.110
And you could take that
parameterized document

00:51:51.110 --> 00:51:53.520
to print it out, to get offline
and do whatever you want.

00:51:53.520 --> 00:51:57.320
Htmlwidgets will build
a self-contained widget.

00:51:57.320 --> 00:52:00.830
When you build an htmlwidget,
it puts in every possibility

00:52:00.830 --> 00:52:02.390
for interaction in that widget.

00:52:02.390 --> 00:52:03.980
If you have a map
widget, it puts

00:52:03.980 --> 00:52:06.110
in the map of the
whole world which

00:52:06.110 --> 00:52:07.610
means you could
take that offline

00:52:07.610 --> 00:52:09.800
and poke around in the
widget and move around.

00:52:09.800 --> 00:52:12.570
And it's going to work for you.

00:52:12.570 --> 00:52:14.960
It's only when you
get to Shiny that you

00:52:14.960 --> 00:52:16.970
need to have an R
session running somewhere

00:52:16.970 --> 00:52:18.770
in the background.

00:52:18.770 --> 00:52:21.380
That R session could
be in a couple places.

00:52:21.380 --> 00:52:22.790
It could be on
your own computer.

00:52:22.790 --> 00:52:25.720
If you have R installed, if you
open the document in RStudio

00:52:25.720 --> 00:52:27.350
and you click Run,
you don't need

00:52:27.350 --> 00:52:28.700
to be connected to the internet.

00:52:28.700 --> 00:52:31.160
RStudio just going to use
your local R session to power

00:52:31.160 --> 00:52:32.000
that Shiny app.

00:52:32.000 --> 00:52:33.848
And it will work for you.

00:52:33.848 --> 00:52:35.390
The second way you
could do it is you

00:52:35.390 --> 00:52:38.570
could put that Shiny app
online either with an RStudio

00:52:38.570 --> 00:52:42.170
Connect, on Shiny server
pro, or in shinyapps.io.

00:52:42.170 --> 00:52:44.780
These are all servers
that are designed

00:52:44.780 --> 00:52:46.790
to run in R session in
the background to power

00:52:46.790 --> 00:52:49.400
their Shiny apps.

00:52:49.400 --> 00:52:51.650
That means that there's an
important use case that you

00:52:51.650 --> 00:52:52.692
need to pay attention to.

00:52:52.692 --> 00:52:55.010
If you're using Shiny,
you need to make sure

00:52:55.010 --> 00:52:57.650
that your server can run an
R session in the background.

00:52:57.650 --> 00:53:00.710
And most web servers aren't
designed to run R sessions.

00:53:00.710 --> 00:53:05.360
So use your options generally
to use one of the three

00:53:05.360 --> 00:53:08.413
I mentioned or also open
source Shiny server.

00:53:08.413 --> 00:53:10.580
So I'll leave that there
because this is bringing us

00:53:10.580 --> 00:53:12.365
to the top of the hour.

00:53:12.365 --> 00:53:14.240
Thank very much, everyone,
for your questions

00:53:14.240 --> 00:53:16.510
and for your attention.

